## Why This Is Asked

Tests error handling discipline, prevents data leakage, and demonstrates practical logging and traceability in a real stack.

## Key Concepts

- Centralized error handling with a dedicated AppError class
- Distinguish log content from client response to avoid leakage
- Environment-based verbosity (prod vs dev)
- RequestId for cross-service tracing
- Consistent HTTP status mapping for common errors

## Code Example

```javascript
class AppError extends Error {
  constructor(message, statusCode) {
    super(message)
    this.statusCode = statusCode
    this.isOperational = true
  }
}

function errorHandler(err, req, res, next) {
  const code = err.statusCode || 500
  const isProd = process.env.NODE_ENV === 'production'
  if (isProd) {
    // Do not leak details
    res.status(code).json({ error: 'Something went wrong', code })
  } else {
    // Development: expose details for debugging
    res.status(code).json({ message: err.message, stack: err.stack, code })
  }
  // Log full details securely (stack, req data, requestId) in both modes
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test error handling across prod/dev? 
- How can you propagate requestId across microservices for end-to-end tracing?

## Why This Is Asked

This tests practical, beginner-friendly steps to mitigate a known-vulnerability in a gateway without full rewrite, emphasizing token lifecycle, key rotation, and dependency governance.

## Key Concepts

- JWT lifetimes and rotation
- Key management and revocation lists
- Gateway security posture
- Continuous integration security gates

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal token verification middleware (Express)
const jwt = require('jsonwebtoken');
const { getCurrentPublicKey, isRevoked } = require('./security');

function verifyToken(req, res, next) {
  const auth = req.headers.authorization || '';
  const token = auth.split(' ')[1];
  if (!token) return res.status(401).send('Unauthorized');
  try {
    const payload = jwt.verify(token, getCurrentPublicKey(), { algorithms: ['RS256'] });
    if (isRevoked(payload.jti)) return res.status(401).send('Revoked');
    req.user = payload;
    next();
  } catch {
    res.status(401).send('Unauthorized');
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you scale revocation checks across multiple gateway instances?
- What are the trade-offs of shorter token lifetimes vs user experience?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical monitoring and defense against credential stuffing in a multi-tenant context, focusing on observable signals, mitigations, and operator visibility.

## Key Concepts

- Per-IP and per-tenant rate limiting
- Sliding window counters
- MFA enforcement for risk
- Centralized logging and alerts
- Synthetic traffic validation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code for login rate limiter using Redis
const { RateLimiterRedis } = require('rate-limiter-flexible');
const redisClient = require('redis').createClient();

const limiter = new RateLimiterRedis({
  points: 5, // attempts
  duration: 60, // per 60s
  storeClient: redisClient
});

// In login route
async function login(req, res) {
  try {
    await limiter.consume(req.ip);
    // authentication logic
    res.send('OK');
  } catch {
    res.status(429).send('Too many attempts');
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you tune thresholds for users behind shared proxies?
- How would you test rate limiter behavior under burst traffic and production latency constraints?

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to translate a security incident into a comprehensive, end-to-end remediation plan that spans logging hygiene, secrets management, memory safety, access controls, and incident response.

## Key Concepts

- Secrets lifecycle management with short lease expirations
- Logging redaction and tamper-evident logging
- In-memory secret handling and secure memory scrubbing
- Per-tenant isolation and least-privilege access controls
- Incident response playbooks, rollback procedures, and post-mortem hardening

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example: redact secrets in logs
function redactSecrets(obj) {
  const SECRET_PATTERNS = [/^(api[_-]?key|secret|token|password)$/i, /vault/i];

  return JSON.stringify(obj, (key, value) => {
    if (SECRET_PATTERNS.some(p => p.test(key))) {
      return '[REDACTED]';
    }
    return value;
  });
}

// Example: secure memory scrubbing
function clearSecretBuffer(buffer) {
  if (buffer && buffer.fill) {
    buffer.fill(0);
  }
}

// Example: tamper-evident log entry
function createLogEntry(secretKey, event, previousHash) {
  const timestamp = Date.now();
  const payload = `${timestamp}|${event}|${secretKey}|${previousHash}`;
  const hash = crypto.createHmac('sha256', process.env.LOG_SECRET)
    .update(payload)
    .digest('hex');

  return { timestamp, event, hash, previousHash };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design secure plugin architectures and threat modeling for untrusted code.

## Key Concepts
- Sandboxing WASM/per-tenant isolation
- Least-privilege API surface
- Code signing and KMS-based key rotation
- Audit/log tamper-evidence
- Incident response with rollback and tenant revocation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Verify plugin signature using Node.js crypto
const crypto = require('crypto');
function verify(sig, payload, publicKey) {
  const verify = crypto.createVerify('SHA256');
  verify.update(payload); verify.end();
  return verify.verify(publicKey, sig, 'base64');
}
```

